An ordinary point of inflection has $f'(x) \neq 0$, and is the steepest point of the curve in that neighbourhood.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.6*\x*\x*\x+0.8*\x});
\draw[dashed] (-1.0,-0.8) -- (1.0,0.8);
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\small] at (0,2.5) {Point of inflection};
\node[font=\footnotesize] at (0,-2.5) {$f''$ changes sign, $f' \neq 0$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+4.8},{0.8*\x*\x*\x});
\draw[dashed] (4.3,0) -- (5.3,0);
\fill (4.8,0) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\small] at (4.8,2.5) {Horizontal inflection};
\node[font=\footnotesize] at (4.8,-2.5) {$f''$ changes sign, $f' = 0$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+9.6},{0.9*\x*\x*\x*\x-0.9});
\draw[dashed] (9.1,-0.9) -- (10.1,-0.9);
\fill (9.6,-0.9) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\small] at (9.6,2.5) {Undulation point};
\node[font=\footnotesize] at (9.6,-2.5) {$f''$ keeps its sign};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
